WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:30.000
2020 is the 100th anniversary of women winning the right to vote in the United States, yet full equality for women in this country remains elusive. Yes, U.S. Senator Kamala Harris is the Democratic nominee for vice president, which is certainly significant. Yet she's only the second black woman ever to be elected to the U.S. Senate, which has only seen 57 female members since the dawn of our country. Only one quarter of the Senate is female today, while the much larger House, with 435 members, is 235 members, is 23,
2020年是美国女性获得投票权100周年，然而在这个国家，女性实现完全平等仍然遥不可及。是的，美国参议员卡玛拉·哈里斯（Kamala Harris）是民主党的副总统候选人，这当然意义重大。然而，她只是美国历史上第二位当选为美国参议员的黑人女性，而自建国以来，参议院总共只有57名女性成员。如今，参议院中只有四分之一是女性，而规模大得多的众议院共有435名成员，其中235名成员，是23，

2
00:00:30.000 --> 00:01:20.000
percent female. The number of women at the top of the business world is also scant. We have just reached an all-time high of 37 women CEOs of the Fortune 500, but that means that almost 93% of those top companies are led by men. Why has it taken so many years for women to make even these gains? What are the particular challenges faced by women who strive to be leaders? How can psychology help us better understand the factors that hold women back or push them down, or discourage them from aiming for the top. Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that explores the connections between psychological science and everyday life. I'm Kim Mills. Our guest today is Dr. Alice Eggley, a professor of psychology at Northwestern
百分之二十三是女性。商业界高层的女性人数也寥寥无几。我们刚刚达到了财富500强中女性首席执行官人数的历史最高点——37人，但这也就意味着这些顶级公司中近93%仍由男性领导。为什么女性花了这么多年才取得这些进展？那些努力成为领导者的女性面临着哪些特殊的挑战？心理学如何能帮助我们更好地理解那些阻碍女性、打压女性，或使她们不敢向高层迈进的因素？欢迎收听《心理学谈话》（Speaking of Psychology），这是美国心理学会的旗舰播客，旨在探索心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。我是金·米尔斯（Kim Mills）。我们今天的嘉宾是西北大学心理学教授爱丽丝·伊格利（Dr. Alice Eagly）博士，

3
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:57.000
University who for many years has studied the psychology of gender, especially sex differences and similarities in leadership, pro-social behavior, aggression, partner preferences, and political attitudes. Dr. Egli has written numerous articles, chapters, and books on these topics. Her most recent book, Through the Labyrinth, The Truth About How Women Become Leaders, challenges the common metaphor of the glass ceiling. Welcome to speaking of psychology, Dr. Egli. I'm pleased to be here, Kim. This is not the first time a woman has been on the vice presidential ticket in this country, and women have run for president, although only once as a major party candidate.
她多年来一直研究性别心理学，特别是领导力、亲社会行为、攻击性、伴侣偏好和政治态度方面的性别差异与相似之处。伊格利博士就这些主题撰写了大量的文章、章节和书籍。她最近的著作《穿越迷宫：女性如何成为领导者的真相》（Through the Labyrinth: The Truth About How Women Become Leaders）挑战了“玻璃天花板”这一常见的隐喻。欢迎来到《心理学谈话》，伊格利博士。很高兴来到这里，金。这并不是美国历史上第一次有女性出现在副总统候选人名单上，女性也曾竞选过总统，尽管作为主要政党候选人只有一次。

4
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Is there something different about what's happening now and are the reactions from the media and the public different this time around? Well, yes. I think that it was entirely expected, even demanded of a Democratic candidate, such as Joe Biden, because the support for the Democratic Party is stronger among women than men. So it really called for women being represented. And then it's a time when there's a good deal of protest and, unrest over minority issues.
现在发生的事情有什么不同吗？媒体和公众的反应这次有什么不同吗？嗯，是的。我认为这对于像乔·拜登（Joe Biden）这样的民主党候选人来说是完全预料之中的，甚至是必须的，因为民主党在女性中的支持率高于男性。所以这确实需要女性代表。而且现在正值人们对少数群体问题有大量抗议和动荡的时期。

5
00:02:34.000 --> 00:03:16.000
And so choosing a woman of color certainly made sense to represent that community, which tends to lean Democratic, but to, you know, to call on their loyalty in this particular way. So I think it was entirely expected and generally very well received. Yeah, that's been interesting that there has not been a whole lot of backlash, I think. No. I mean, what are the characteristics that make her less of a target? She is quite qualified in the sense that she had a number of political roles in California, and she is a senator.
因此，选择一位有色人种女性来代表那个倾向于支持民主党的群体确实很有意义，你知道，这是一种以这种特殊方式呼吁她们忠诚的做法。所以我认为这是完全预料之中的，而且总体上非常受欢迎。是的，有趣的是，我认为并没有出现太多的强烈反对。没有。我的意思是，是什么特征使她受到的攻击较少？她在某种意义上非常有资格，因为她在加利福尼亚州担任过多个政治职务，而且她是一位参议员。

6
00:03:16.000 --> 00:04:22.000
And so she has very good qualifications that would be normal in a male candidate. So she's qualified in that sense. she's also quite gifted as a speaker and a debater, as was displayed earlier in the hearings in the Senate. So I think she struck the public as, you know, somebody who had the toughness to be a good campaigner. So what particular challenges do women politicians face first as candidates and then once they're elected? Well, there are serious challenges that are embedded in the culture, in a sense, in terms of the way we think about leaders and the way we think about women and men. So this is well established in studies on stereotyping done by psychologists and political scientists in this case. So we know that people think women are the nicer, kind, or more compassionate sex, more sympathetic to elements.
所以她具备男性候选人通常会有的非常好的资历。从这个意义上说，她是合格的。她作为演讲者和辩论者也非常有天赋，正如早些时候在参议院听证会上所展示的那样。所以我认为她给公众留下的印象是，你知道，一个有韧性成为一名优秀竞选者的人。那么，女性政治家首先作为候选人，然后一旦当选，会面临哪些特殊的挑战呢？嗯，在某种意义上，这些挑战深深植根于文化之中，体现在我们思考领导者的方式以及我们思考女性和男性的方式上。这一点在心理学家和政治学家所做的刻板印象研究中已经得到了很好的证实。所以我们知道，人们认为女性是更友善、更和蔼或更富有同情心的性别，对

7
00:04:22.000 --> 00:05:01.000
others, better social skills, and the tougher, more agentic, we call it, more assertive, more aggressive sex. Okay, we know about that. But then people think about leaders as more agentic than communal. So they think about, you know, they have to take charge, they have to be tough, tell people what to do often. So that image is widely shared in the culture and is much more similar to men than women. So that puts women at something of a disadvantage.
他人更具同情心，社交能力更强；而男性是更强硬、更具代理性（我们称之为agentic）、更果断、更具攻击性的性别。好的，我们知道这一点。但人们又认为领导者比女性更具代理性，而不是更具公共性。所以他们认为，你知道，他们必须掌控全局，他们必须强硬，经常告诉别人该做什么。因此，这种形象在文化中被广泛认同，而且与男性的形象比女性更相似。这使女性处于某种劣势。

8
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:34.000
And it sort of goes like that. Well, she's, you know, she's very nice. But then the butt is, you know, I don't know if she's tough enough to handle that kind of situation. Could she really hold up in a big debate, you know? Could she get people to do things? Which she have the respect and authority. And so that lingers in the culture. And so women have to overcome, in a sense, these anxieties people have.
情况往往是这样的：嗯，她，你知道，她人很好。但随之而来的“但是”是，你知道，我不知道她是否足够强硬来处理那种情况。她真的能在大型辩论中坚持住吗？你知道，她能让人们去做事吗？她会有威望和权威吗？这种想法在文化中挥之不去。因此，女性在某种意义上必须克服人们的这些焦虑。

9
00:05:34.000 --> 00:06:20.000
It's not about being a good person or a competent person. It's about being a tough enough person to really take on that kind of role that demands a lot of competition and assertion. And yet, if you're too, tough as a woman, then that cuts against you as well. That's right. That's the agency paradox. So, if you're very assertive, like, so you say, well, okay, I'll be just like a man. Wrong. That doesn't really work for women. Unless, you know, it could take on qualities of being assertive to some extent, but not to go to the extremes as dangerous, but add to it the qualities of warmth and kindness and empathy.
这与是否是一个好人或有能力的人无关。这关乎是否足够强硬，能够真正承担起那种需要大量竞争和主张的角色。然而，如果你作为一个女性太强硬，那也会对你不利。没错。这就是“代理悖论”。所以，如果你非常果断，比如你说，好吧，我会像个男人一样。错了。这对女性来说并不奏效。除非，你知道，可以在某种程度上具备果断的品质，但不要走向极端，那很危险，还要加上温暖、善良和同理心的品质。

10
00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:52.000
So display both. That tends to be something that works for women who are leaders or wish to be leaders. And you can see that in Kamala Harris right away. She speaks in a clear, pleasantly assertive way. she smiles a lot too and has a lot of feminine expressiveness in her gestures and voice. Not too much, but it's definitely there.
所以要两者兼顾。这往往对那些已经是领导者或希望成为领导者的女性有效。你可以立刻在卡玛拉·哈里斯身上看到这一点。她说话清晰、令人愉快且果断。她也经常微笑，在手势和声音中有很多女性化的表达。不是太多，但绝对存在。

11
00:06:52.000 --> 00:07:17.000
And so I think she has that blend that, in fact, works very well for women in leadership roles. You recently published a study that looked at a large body of nationally representative public opinion polling and found that women are now perceived as being just as competent as men as well as more communal, but they're seen as less ambitious. How have these views changed over the past decades?
所以我认为她拥有那种实际上对担任领导职务的女性非常有效的融合特质。你最近发表了一项研究，审视了大量具有全国代表性的公众舆论调查，发现女性现在被认为与男性一样有能力，而且更具公共性，但她们被认为野心较小。这些观点在过去几十年里发生了怎样的变化？

12
00:07:17.000 --> 00:08:04.000
Is this good news for women leaders today? It's mixed news. So this study was based on representative public opinion poll data where people were asked about men and women. And the data go back to 1946 and then through 2018. So it's quite a nice series. And so the way the pollsters do this is they ask a very direct question. They say like intelligence, more true of women, more true of men, or equally true. And so we do see a lot of change in that women's competence relative to men has risen.
这对今天的女性领导者来说是好消息吗？这是喜忧参半的消息。这项研究基于具有代表性的民意调查数据，人们被问及关于男性和女性的问题。数据可以追溯到1946年，一直到2018年。所以这是一个非常好的系列。民意调查人员这样做的方式是提出一个非常直接的问题。他们会问，比如智力，是女性更具备，男性更具备，还是同样具备。我们确实看到了很多变化，女性相对于男性的能力有所提升。

13
00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:44.000
So most Americans now say equal. But those who don't say equal are more likely to say women than men. So there. Competence leans slightly in the female direction. So that would be like being intelligent. The other change is rather unexpected to many observers, I think, or social scientists in particular, that the communion of women versus men has increased. So now it's overwhelmingly true, if you say something like sympathetic, more true of women or men.
所以大多数美国人现在说“平等”。但那些不说“平等”的人，认为女性比男性更具备能力的比例更高。所以，能力稍微向女性倾斜。这就像是聪明才智。另一个变化对许多观察家，特别是社会科学家来说是相当出乎意料的，那就是女性相对于男性的公共性增加了。所以现在，如果你问类似“富有同情心”的问题，是女性还是男性更具备，答案绝大多数是女性。

14
00:08:44.000 --> 00:09:29.000
It's women. So that has changed over time to be more extreme. And agency doesn't change. So 1946 people saw men as more aggressive and assertive than women. And they still do, and there's no significant change in that trend. So for women not to be troubled by these stereotypes, it's the agency that has to change as well as the competence. People have to see women as tough enough, and that still goes in the male direction. The competence is very helpful, you know, if anything, you know, women are somewhat more competent than men, because competence is a, you know, something people expect of leaders.
是女性。所以这一点随着时间的推移变得更加极端。而代理性并没有改变。所以1946年人们认为男性比女性更具攻击性和果断。他们现在仍然这样认为，这一趋势没有显著变化。因此，女性要摆脱这些刻板印象，不仅能力需要改变，代理性也需要改变。人们必须认为女性足够强硬，而这一点仍然向男性倾斜。能力非常有帮助，你知道，如果说有什么不同的话，女性在某种程度上比男性更有能力，因为能力是人们对领导者的期望。

15
00:09:29.000 --> 00:10:16.000
And the communion is probably not particularly effective in terms of being seen as even more communal than women in the past. Well, what do you think is different in other countries? I mean, women are still underrepresented in leadership in America, but other industrialized countries have been led by women. You can think of Germany or England or even New Zealand today. What's different there? The political scientists explain that women have better chances at a parliamentary system than a presidential system. So in a parliamentary system, you know, the executive and the legislative branches are combined because the prime minister is the head of the party that wins.
而公共性在被视为比过去更具公共性方面可能并不是特别有效。那么，你认为其他国家有什么不同？我的意思是，女性在美国的领导层中仍然代表性不足，但其他工业化国家已经由女性领导。你可以想到今天的德国、英国甚至新西兰。那里有什么不同？政治学家解释说，女性在议会制国家比在总统制国家有更好的机会。在议会制国家，你知道，行政和立法部门是结合在一起的，因为总理是获胜政党的领袖。

16
00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:44.000
It has the majority. And so women can make their way up through good service and popularity in their own districts up the hierarchy in the party, you know, like Margaret Thatcher and a couple of others, and you can make it to the top of your party, and then, by the way, you get to be prime minister if your party is elected. They don't elect a person directly. They elect a party. But we don't have that.
它拥有多数席位。所以女性可以通过在自己选区良好的服务和受欢迎程度，在党内层级中晋升，你知道，就像玛格丽特·撒切尔（Margaret Thatcher）和其他几个人一样，你可以进入党内高层，然后，顺便说一句，如果你的政党当选，你就能成为总理。他们不是直接选举一个人。他们选举一个政党。但我们没有那样。

17
00:10:44.000 --> 00:11:32.000
We have the separate executive. And so the focus is all on this one role. And, you know, being a woman or a man, you're voting for the person. And I think that brings out the difficulties that women have even in the UK, because they're never voted for as a person when they get to be prime minister. And then the other thing, in some countries, and it happened a little bit here as an attempt, you know, there's a passing on through family, very elite family ties. And so Indira Gandhi was Nero's daughter. Right. And so some countries have that passing on.
我们有独立的行政部门。所以焦点全在这个单一的角色上。而且，你知道，作为一个女性或男性，你是在为这个人投票。我认为这带来了女性即使在英国也面临的困难，因为当她们成为总理时，她们从来不是作为一个人被投票选出的。还有一件事，在一些国家，这里也发生过一点尝试，你知道，通过家庭传承，非常精英的家庭纽带。所以英迪拉·甘地（Indira Gandhi）是尼赫鲁（Nehru）的女儿。没错。所以一些国家有那种传承。

18
00:11:32.000 --> 00:12:01.000
And if there's not a male to pass it on to it, it might get passed on to a wife in some cases or, you know, to a child. Of course, we had a hint of that with the Clintons, didn't we? Almost. But it didn't. go and all was said and done. But it's not a tradition in the U.S. to do that. Exactly. There was the two bushes, so it looked like we did it, though.
如果没有男性可以传承，在某些情况下可能会传给妻子，或者，你知道，传给孩子。当然，我们在克林顿夫妇身上看到了这一点，不是吗？几乎。但它没有成功，一切都结束了。但这在美国并不是一种传统。确实。有两个布什，所以看起来我们做到了，尽管如此。

19
00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:27.000
But anyway, it's a stronger tradition in some other countries where it's happened at an earlier point, you know, as when Indira Gandhi was Prime Minister of India. So there are differences in the governmental system. There may be differences in attitudes, too, but it's particularly the structure of the political system, I think. I mean, we would tend to see some of those cultures as being even more sexish than the United States. Yes, yes.
但无论如何，在一些更早发生这种情况的国家，这是一种更强的传统，你知道，就像英迪拉·甘地担任印度总理时那样。所以政府体制存在差异。态度可能也存在差异，但我认为主要是政治体制的结构。我的意思是，我们倾向于认为其中一些文化比美国更性别歧视。是的，是的。

20
00:12:27.000 --> 00:13:09.000
But yet, like Pakistan and India and other countries have had. Right, Benazir Budo, yeah, same situation. Yeah, have had women. And they tend to have been closely aligned in the narrow elites that were in the, you know, often by blood. So in your book through the labyrinth, you challenge the notion of the glass ceiling, that solid but somewhat invisible barrier that most of us think women face when it comes to reaching the highest echelons of leadership. Why do you think that metaphor is not apt and that a labyrinth is closer to the challenges that women face? Well, there's a couple of reasons at least. One is it suggests a very solid barrier.
但像巴基斯坦、印度和其他国家却有过。没错，贝娜齐尔·布托（Benazir Bhutto），是的，同样的情况。是的，有过女性领导人。而且她们往往与狭隘的精英阶层紧密结盟，你知道，通常是血缘关系。在你的书《穿越迷宫》中，你挑战了“玻璃天花板”的概念，即我们大多数人认为女性在达到最高领导层时面临的那种坚固但有些隐形的障碍。为什么你认为这个隐喻不恰当，而“迷宫”更接近女性面临的挑战？嗯，至少有几个原因。首先，它暗示了一个非常坚固的障碍。

21
00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:53.000
And I don't think it's like prejudices aren't like that. They are more fungible and malleable. And it isn't that we've never had women as, for instance, CEOs in the Fortune 500. So there are some and have been others. But the other reason, it suggests that the barrier is right near the top, right? So you move up through your career and, oh, my God, you can't. be CEO because there's this barrier at the top, next to the top. But that's not how it works in terms of careers in management or other areas that involve leadership. The challenges are
我不认为偏见是那样的。它们更具可变性和可塑性。并不是说我们从未有过女性担任财富500强公司的首席执行官。所以有一些，也曾经有过其他人。但另一个原因是，它暗示障碍就在顶端附近，对吧？所以你在职业生涯中向上攀升，然后，天哪，你不能成为首席执行官，因为顶端有这个障碍。但管理或其他涉及领导力的领域的职业生涯并不是这样运作的。挑战是

22
00:13:53.000 --> 00:14:47.000
all along the way. It's in day one. You go to work as a, you know, a manager just coming in with your MBA. The challenges you have as a woman are there and they're there every day. And As you rise, there are different challenges, you know, it goes on and on. So it isn't as if you're exactly the same as men, and then near the top there's a glass ceiling. It's really a completely wrong metaphor. You know, it has some truth to it, perhaps, but it doesn't adequately represent the nature of the challenges, which are all along the way. And so that's why we propose labyrinth, that the man has a relatively straight road. You know, maybe he has some bumps along the way or whatever.
一路走来都有。从第一天开始。你作为一名刚拿到MBA学位的经理去工作。你作为女性面临的挑战就在那里，而且每天都在那里。随着你的晋升，会有不同的挑战，你知道，它没完没了。所以并不是说你和男性完全一样，然后在顶端附近有一个玻璃天花板。这真的是一个完全错误的隐喻。你知道，它可能有一些道理，但它不能充分代表挑战的本质，这些挑战是贯穿始终的。这就是为什么我们提出“迷宫”，男人有一条相对笔直的道路。你知道，也许他一路上有一些颠簸之类的。

23
